# Security Rules

Security is designed into every layer. Follow `docs/SECURITY.md` for the full threat model and control details.

## Row Level Security Required

- RLS must be enabled on every tenant-scoped table before the migration merges.
- RLS is the last line of defense; application checks provide UX, RLS provides enforcement.
- Test cross-tenant access attempts in integration tests — they must fail.
- Policy changes are security-critical and require explicit review.

## Never Trust Client workspace_id

- Derive workspace context server-side from authenticated membership, URL slug validation, or session token claims.
- Treat `workspace_id` sent from the client (request body, query params, headers) as untrusted input.
- Every query against tenant data must filter by a server-validated `workspace_id`.
- Service-role queries must include an explicit `workspace_id` parameter; never query tenant tables without a workspace filter.

## Validate All Input

- Validate every API input with Zod schemas before processing (see backend rules).
- Use `strict()` mode; disable unsafe type coercion.
- Enforce string length limits, UUID format, and email validation at the schema level.
- Strip null bytes and control characters from user-provided text.
- Messages are plain text in MVP — no HTML from visitors.

## No Service Role in Browser

- `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` is used exclusively in server context (Route Handlers, Server Actions, cron jobs).
- Never import service-role clients in Client Components, hooks, or any code bundled for the browser.
- Only the Supabase anon key and project URL are client-exposed; they rely on RLS for protection.
- CI must fail builds that detect the service role key in client bundles.

## Rate Limiting

- Apply rate limits to public and widget API endpoints to prevent abuse.
- Widget endpoints (see `docs/SECURITY.md` Section 5.3):
  - `POST /widget/init`: 30 req/min per IP
  - `POST /widget/messages`: 60 req/min per session
  - `POST /widget/attachments`: 10 req/min per session
- Include rate limit headers in responses (`X-RateLimit-Remaining`).
- Authenticated dashboard endpoints should have appropriate per-user or per-workspace limits.

## Secure File Uploads

- Validate file size against plan limits before accepting uploads.
- Detect MIME type via magic bytes (`file-type`), not the `Content-Type` header alone.
- Sanitize filenames; reject path traversal characters.
- Whitelist allowed types: JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP, PDF, plain text, CSV.
- Store files under workspace-scoped paths in Supabase Storage with RLS policies.

## OWASP Recommendations

Apply OWASP Top 10 mitigations throughout the codebase:

| Risk | Mitigation |
|------|------------|
| Broken access control | RLS + middleware + role checks; deny by default |
| Cryptographic failures | TLS everywhere; secrets in env vars only; hash tokens at rest |
| Injection | Parameterized queries via Supabase client; no dynamic SQL in app code |
| Insecure design | Threat model in `docs/SECURITY.md`; defense in depth |
| Security misconfiguration | Security headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options); least-privilege env scoping |
| Vulnerable components | Dependabot; patch critical/high CVEs within 48 hours; frozen lockfile in CI |
| Auth failures | Supabase Auth with refresh rotation; short-lived JWTs; explicit logout |
| Software/data integrity | Stripe webhook signature verification; idempotent webhook processing |
| Logging failures | Audit logs for security events; Sentry for error monitoring; never log secrets |
| SSRF | Validate and allowlist external URLs; no user-controlled server-side fetches without review |

## Widget-Specific Security

- The widget runs on untrusted third-party sites. Treat all widget input as hostile.
- Validate `Origin` / `Referer` against the workspace domain allowlist on every widget API request.
- Use iframe isolation with restrictive sandbox attributes.
- Validate `postMessage` origins before processing cross-frame communication.

## Secrets and Headers

- Never commit secrets, API keys, or `.env.local` files.
- Apply HTTP security headers via Next.js middleware (see `docs/SECURITY.md` Section 7.2).
- Dashboard API is same-origin only; widget API CORS allows only allowlisted origins.
